# Cursor rules — SteadyCut

## Your lane (cost-split with Claude Code)
You IMPLEMENT decided specs. You do NOT redesign, re-architect, or pick algorithm
parameters. If a task needs "why is this happening" or "which value is right" —
STOP and leave a `# TODO(claude):` note instead of guessing.

- Specs live in `for_claude_sessions/specs/SPEC-*.md` (active). Implement exactly what the spec
  says. Ignore `specs/done/` — those are already merged (archive, do not re-implement).
- Touch only the files the spec names. If you need a 3rd file, flag it, don't sprawl.
- After implementing: run the spec's acceptance test, report pass/fail. Don't mark done on a guess.

## Running on Auto (default model)
Cursor runs on **Auto** to save cost. Auto is strong on SMALL, well-defined tasks; weak on
system design. That's fine here — every spec is pre-scoped to Auto's strengths.
- If a prompt would be "make it better" / "design X" / "figure out the approach" → it's too big
  for Auto. Stop, leave `# TODO(claude):`, and let Claude design it (or the user pins Sonnet).
- One spec = one task = one branch. Never batch.

## Prompting Auto (templates — copy/paste)
**Implement a spec:**
> Implement `for_claude_sessions/specs/SPEC-<x>.md` exactly. Follow .cursorrules. Branch first per
> the handoff section. Run the spec's acceptance test, report pass/fail. Only the named files.

**Tiny tweak (no spec needed):**
> In `<file>:<line>`, change `<X>` to `<Y>` because `<reason>`. Don't touch anything else.
> Show me the diff and confirm it still imports/runs.

**If Auto drifts (sprawls, redesigns, argues with spec):**
> Stop. Re-read .cursorrules. Implement only what the spec says, only the named files.

## Project facts
- Python 3.11 target. Entry GUI: `run.py` (pywebview + FastAPI). CLI: `steadycut_pipeline.py:main()`.
- Pipeline core: `pipelinev3.py` (motion/windows), `shot_classifier.py` (YOLO), `xml_assembler.py` (FCP7 XML out).
- ffmpeg via `ffmpeg_helper.py` (bundled→cache→PATH→download). Never hardcode ffmpeg paths.
- Build: `./build.sh` (needs a venv providing `python`/`pip`; system only has python3/pip3).
- Output is FCP7 XML imported into Premiere — XML shape changes MUST be tested by real import.

## Don't
- Don't regenerate proxies, don't touch `build.yml` CI order (torch CPU-first is load-bearing).
- Don't reformat whole files. Match surrounding style.

## Branch handoff (one branch per spec → Claude reviews → merge)
Before starting a spec:
    git checkout main && git pull
    git checkout -b cursor/<spec-slug>      # e.g. cursor/f5-version-label
Implement the spec, then run its acceptance test. Commit only the files the spec named:
    git add <named files>
    git commit -m "<spec-slug>: <what changed>"
    git push -u origin cursor/<spec-slug>
Then hand back to Claude Code for review (paste the branch name, or in Claude run):
    git fetch && git checkout cursor/<spec-slug>
    git diff main...HEAD
Claude reviews. On pass:
    git checkout main && git merge --no-ff cursor/<spec-slug> && git branch -d cursor/<spec-slug>
Never merge your own branch to main — Claude reviews first. One spec = one branch = one PR-sized diff.
